A||N||8||Ozzy||'87 GT runs colder than it should!!||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||04-28-2000||03:12 PM||||Hey Fiero Fans...<P>I recently changed out the old thermostat to a FS 165 deg 'stat. Fixed the cooling fan too. (SOme bonehead had hot wired it wrong and burnt up the control head circut board trace) Anyway, engine temp seemed fine on the guage but recently the engine temp seems to be much cooler than it should even with the lower temp 'stat.<P>I'm VERY aware of the sending unit and guage problems with Fiero's. Assuming these are working and the thermostat IS functioning correctly (which I will verify tonight) is there any other common problems you guys / gals had along similar lines???<P><BR>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||04-28-2000||03:39 PM||||Could be that you're not to hot of a mechanic. On a more important note I think that you may be doing yourself a disservice. If you run the 165 thermostat you are supposed to change the chip to deal with the lower operating temp. Someone with more ECM savvy please jump in  - think about 185 is as low you can go without chip changes||||reg||1
Z||000002||triker||04-28-2000||04:00 PM||||I believe Phil is right about running too low a thermistat causing performance probs.  Somewhere along the line I read that the Fiero ECM is still in the 'choke' mode until the engine temp hits about 195.  After discussing engine temps with a couple of mechanics and an old time radiator repairman, I went back to the stock 195 thermistat from the 180.  The concenses was that an internal cumbustion engine performs best at about 215 degrees f.  Of course you want as cold of air as possible going into the manifold.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||04-28-2000||04:23 PM||||My GT is running a 180 that I installed.  It's just perfect.  Easy and cheap fix. ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Ozzy||04-28-2000||06:31 PM||||Phil...Not to hot of a mechanic??? Thnx fer the vote of confidence. I guess if I was, I would not have to ask any questions here huh?<P>Other than that...(as Ozzy rolls over the floor recovering from a sharp stab in the side!) Terryk is the guy to answer the ECM questions for sure.<P>I'm saying that the car still operated around 193 deg to 180 deg according to my scanner last week. Now the temp appears to be running significantly lower and not because of the coolant fan. (According to the gauge that is)<P>I will check the scanner out again this weekend. It may just be the sending unit or gauge.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||04-29-2000||02:32 AM||||A 180 will work fine, the engine usually runs at 10-15 degrees higher than the stat setting.  I'm running a 180 and my engine runs great.  it's usually in the 200 range when I'm stuck in traffic, anyways||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-29-2000||03:31 PM||||None of the ECM's care about a 180. A 165 however can keep them(stock ECM) from running closed loop when they should be. not good. You can cook off the catalyst like that.<P>You can verify all of what is going on with a scanner.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ozzy||04-30-2000||12:03 AM||||Thnx ogre and all the humans too!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I have some updates...<P>Took my scanner and plugged in this morning. Also made some Thermostat soufle...hot water and thermometer. 'stat is opening at right temp. I verified the the ECM is seeing 165 deg and is operating in closed loop mode. The problem is the gauge again! I don't know that it was bad before but I dismantled the dash last week to place some pins on the gauge face. That was succesful but maybe there is a poor connection on the trace connector or something. I checked the gauge with resistors (1385 ohms/100f and 55 ohms/260f) It was way off. <P>I have it "calibrated" now. I kinda cheated though. The sending unit checked out fine with respect to the resistances in the Haynes schematics so I took a potentiometer (250k) and placed it in series between the gauge sending unit pin and ground. Looking at the scanner I could see what temp the ECM is seeing and adjusted the pot untill the gauge corellated with the ECM temp reading. Then reading the resistance setting on the pot (152.3 ohms) I dug out a 150 ohm resistor and made a jumper to go between ground, thru the resistor and to gauge pin of the sending unit. I watched the gauge cycle with the fan and it stays accurate thru it's range.<P>I know this is not really the way I should fix this since I do so much yellin' about shortcut, sloppy work but it is only temporary 'till I can find time to pull the dash and check the gauge out again.<P>Ozzy<P>P.S. I don't have a catylist. When I repaired my exhaust mysteriously the cat. fell off and was replaced by a piece of shiny new 2" pipe!! I just have no idea how... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 04-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||04-30-2000||02:58 AM||||Ozzy, if you plan to come to Utah you may have to find the cat.that "fell" off.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>(I did the short version of catalytic converter because I don't know how to spell it)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 04-30-2000).]||||reg||1
